Q: DDD: Modeling simple domain with two aggregate roots A: <blockquote>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Get all bids posted by specific member&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Get all bids for specific item&quot;.</li>
</ul>
<p>So taking into account that I need to implement both of these use cases in my application, what is the right and efficient way to model this domain then?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I usually prefer to follow <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/19620404/entity-vs-dto-in-cqrs/19625738#19625738">this CQRS-diagram</a>. You're right, Entity Model and Domain Model are different Layers. First you use in DataAccess Layer the second is description of your BusinessLogics, applied for Commands transfering data to Persistence Layer and/or Presentation in your UI (via Queries). And you use <strong>methods from Repository Layer</strong> to make anything you like with your data. Your <em>GetAllBidsForMember</em> &amp; <em>GetAllBidsForItem</em> methods you can do here. Whether you will make BusinessLogicModelLayer &amp; RepositoryLayer separately or in 1 project it's up to you. Anyway you can then use these methods in your Controller to get Queries (referencing your DAL in Repository Layer, the last in BusinessLogics (Domain) Layer (dll), which then can use in your Presentation Layer). Or for easier maintainance can use DependencyInjection and Inversion-Of-Control containers (according the DependencyInversion principle).</p>
<p>Repository is the right place to use <strong>ORM</strong> for your queris and commands. Though you can separate <em>query-handlers</em> and <em>command-handlers</em> to different microservices, as in CQRS architectural design pattern (to synchronize actions from different users by Event-subscription).</p>
<p>With GenericRepository:</p>
<pre><code>        public virtual T GetSingle(Func&lt;T, bool&gt; where,
             params System.Linq.Expressions.Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, object&gt;&gt;[] navigationProperties)
        {
            T item = null;
            using (var context = new myEntities())
            {
                IQueryable&lt;T&gt; dbQuery = context.Set&lt;T&gt;();

                //Apply eager loading
                foreach (System.Linq.Expressions.Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, object&gt;&gt; navigationProperty in navigationProperties)
                    dbQuery = dbQuery.Include&lt;T, object&gt;(navigationProperty);

                item = dbQuery
                    .AsNoTracking() //Don't track any changes for the selected item
                    .FirstOrDefault(where); //Apply where clause
            }
            return item;
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Then can just do your BusinessLayer with methods:</p>
<pre><code>        public Bid GetBidsByItem(string ItemName)
        {
            return _deptRepository.GetSingle(
                d =&gt; d.Item.Equals(ItemName), 
                    d =&gt; d.Bid); //include related bids
        }
</code></pre>
<p>If in Query-Handler you want  query just with SQL - can see xhafan's answer.</p>
<p>According to <strong>Clean Architecture</strong>:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/53VSiODH.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/53VSiODH.png" alt="Clean Architecture" /></a></p>
<p>If you want to keep your core business logic completely independent of database technologies, Repository interface you define in DomainLayer and Infrastucture Layer contains the concrete implementations of the repository interfaces (e.g., entity framework, database contexts, API calls), while Application Layer (The Orchestrator) uses the repository interfaces to execute use cases. Application Layer acts as a bridge but doesn't define the contract/interface. Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) are commonly defined in the application layer, or simply ViewModels for Presentation Layer.</p>
<p>P.S.</p>
<p>If you reference your question to &quot;Pro ASP.NET MVC 3 Framework book by Apress&quot; book - you should have read following examples of <strong>extention-methods</strong>. Some developers prefer to make <em>extension classes</em> for already created closed-dlls/services (according to Open-Closed Principle):</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensionMethods {
    // Extends the decimal type to include a TotalPrice calculation
    public static decimal TotalPrices(this ShoppingCart cartParam) {
        decimal total = 0;
        foreach (Product prod in cartParam.Products) {
            total += prod.Price;
        }
        return total;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>P.P.S.</p>
<p>Concerning event-handlers in the linked CQRS-diagram - can use simple POCOs for Data-Driven approach (in MVVM for WPF), Event Sourcing as back-end for async EDA (with ajax in front-end), or any Message Broker (like Kafka etc) in more complex projects with multiple microservices.</p>

Q: How to receive and send SMS (Kotlin Android Studio) A: <p>First you need to request some permissions, put this in your <strong>Manifest.xml</strong> file:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;uses-feature
        android:name=&quot;android.hardware.telephony&quot;
        android:required=&quot;true&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;uses-permission android:name=&quot;android.permission.READ_SMS&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;uses-permission android:name=&quot;android.permission.RECEIVE_SMS&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Than you can use this code directly in your Fragment to receive and send SMSs:</p>
<pre><code>//This code to check if permissions are granted (used in fragment) than request them if not
if (activity?.checkSelfPermission(android.Manifest.permission.RECEIVE_SMS) != PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED
|| activity?.checkSelfPermission(android.Manifest.permission.READ_SMS) != PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED
|| activity?.checkSelfPermission(android.Manifest.permission.SEND_SMS) != PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED
) {
    activity?.requestPermissions(
        arrayOf(android.Manifest.permission.RECEIVE_SMS,
            android.Manifest.permission.SEND_SMS,
            android.Manifest.permission.READ_SMS), PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED
    )
}

//And this code to wait for and recive SMS
val br = object : BroadcastReceiver() {
    override fun onReceive(p0: Context?, p1: Intent?) {
        for (sms in Telephony.Sms.Intents.getMessagesFromIntent(
            p1
        )) {
            val smsSender = sms.originatingAddress
            val smsMessageBody = sms.displayMessageBody
            if (smsSender == &quot;the_number_that_you_expect_the_SMS_to_come_FROM&quot;) {
                binding.textView.text = smsMessageBody
                break
            }
        }
    }
}

//register this broadcast receiver here
registerReceiver(
requireContext(),
br,
IntentFilter(&quot;android.provider.Telephony.SMS_RECEIVED&quot;),
RECEIVER_EXPORTED
)

//This function for sending SMS
fun sendSms(phoneNumber: String, message: String) {
    val smsManager = SmsManager.getDefault()
    smsManager.sendTextMessage(phoneNumber, null, message, null, null)
}
</code></pre>
